{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1451650-1940s-hypertech","id":1451650,"title":"1940s Hypertech","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-06-11T06:33:45-07:00","id":1451650,"title":"1940s Hypertech","content":"Hi!<br /> <br /> As usual, I come here looking for ideas and inspiration. <br /> <br /> Im running a historical mage story set in the 1940s and it has been difficult for me to imagine the kind of gadgets and hypertech the Technocracy might have had back then. Grasping steampunk-like/victorian stuff seems too out of date, while anything digital seems too futuristic (I wouldn't like to break the historical mood that much). <br /> <br /> Any ideas would be greatly appreciated.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5394-argonot","name":"Argonot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5394_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"227"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T06:45:15-07:00","id":1451653,"title":"","content":"Check out the writings of the Golden Age of Science Fiction: Robert Heinlein, Isaac Asimov, Arthur Clark, and their like.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T08:42:28-07:00","id":1451670,"title":"","content":"Am I right that this is more about aesthetics than about capabilities?<br /> <br /> A general rule of thumb when figuring out Technocracy aesthetics is that it is a mix of what technology looks like at the time with what people (sleeper and Technocrat) think it will look like in 20-50 years. You can get an idea of what people of the 40's thought the future would look like by searching for 40's scifi cover art. <br /> <br /> When new things are introduced they often start out looking like old things. The earliest cars looked a lot like carriages. The earliest plastic phones were made to look like traditional wood and metal ones. Only after the new thing becomes normal do people begin experimenting with new shapes and appearances. <br /> <br /> I'll add one other suggestion and that is that the early 40s is part of WWII so expect things to have a certain utilitarian/surplus aesthetic. On the other hand the late 40s was the start of the cold war and paranoid espionage. Expect tech from then to be subtle and hidden.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-12T07:05:41-07:00","id":1451841,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1451650-1940s-hypertech?p=1451653#post1451653\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Check out the writings of the Golden Age of Science Fiction: Robert Heinlein, Isaac Asimov, Arthur Clark, and their like.</div> </div> </div> </div>More information: the genres are retroactively labeled dieselpunk and atompunk, because naturally every SFF sub-genre has to be extraneously called something entirely different and suffixed with &quot;-punk&quot; regardless how, what, why, content, context, or theme.<br /> <br /> As much as I loathe to call it out, thematically the Fallout games would be a good start, minus the post-apocalyptic nature of the setting. The cumbersome, bulky, ostentatious powered armors that go hand-in-hand with the setting would be entirely in It-X's and the VE's wheelhouse. But beyond that, the smart move <i>would</i> be for things to be digital and powered by transistors and microprocessors; the rule of thumb with hypertech in the post-industrial era, is for it to function equivalently to technology fifty years ahead of its period, or a semi-reasonable analog in non-historical settings but with a mishmash of that era's aesthetic and ideas of what technology would be.<br /> <br /> So, a good example of a 1940's hypertech device would be a...cell phone. Just a cell phone, no bells or whistles, certainly no Technocractic intranet connectivity but maybe agents with a high degree of technical expertise could send SMS. Except, one of the early- to mid-'90s satchel phones, and instead of having a nickel-cadmium battery, being powered by a miniature radiothermal generator. And of course, because cell towers don't exist yet, it would instead use a Correspondence effect to connect to real-life operators -- likely Extraordinary Citizens -- who would patch agents into the existing telephone system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":431,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","user":"Theodrim","date":". 06-12-2021, 10:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-12T20:41:13-07:00","id":1451937,"title":"","content":"The biggest influence is probably going to be the &quot;Golden Age&quot; of Science Fiction, generally centered around John W. Campbell and his editorship of Astounding Science Fiction magazine. He tended to prefer &quot;hard&quot; science fiction stories, based on various scientific theories and technologies of the day, and the celebration of scientific progress and what said progress would enable humanity to accomplish. (The flip side of this is the same period's dystopian works influenced by what was going on in the Soviet Union, Germany, Italy, and Spain. This includes Aldous Huxley's Brave New World (1936), George Orwell's 1984 (1948), and Alexander Korda's adaption of HG Wells's Things to Come (1936), as well as earlier works like Yevegeny Zamyatin's We (1920) and Fritz Lang's Metropolis (1927).)<br /> <br /> From an aesthetic perspective, this is the period when Art Deco had transitioned into Streamline Moderne and is now (after the War) developing Mid-Century Modern and Googie styles. (Googie is especially interesting as it was heavily influenced by the period's development of car culture, jets, and the Atomic Age.) Again, the flip side of this are the Brutalist, Stalinist, and PWA Moderne. If you plug any of those terms into Google Images (or its equivalent) along with &quot;design&quot; or &quot;architecture&quot;, you can get a good idea of a lot of the overall design style. <br /> <br /> As far as technology, everything is Analog. This is a world of transistors, vacuum tubes, and slide rules, as well as new synthetic materials (such as nylon and plexiglass) and computing machines that took up entire rooms (if not entire buildings). This will continue to be a thing for a while, even in science fiction. If you've ever seen the earliest appearances of Iron Man in the early 60s, all his technology for his suit is based entirely on the &quot;magic&quot; of transistors and magnets. <br /> <br /> The single signature device for Technocracy agents throughout the 30s and 40s (and even into the 60s, to be honest) is the Dick Tracy Two-Way Wrist Radio. How you have this item work is up to you. Correspondence is one option, as is Mind, or possibly even Forces. It may also be an almost mundane device constructed by taking advantage of Matter to make the parts small enough to actually work and Forces or Prime for a power source. <br /> Other ideas I'd suggest would be to borrow heavily from other 30s and 40s pulp works, including radio and serials. This includes Green Hornet's gas pistol and his Black Beauty car (it's main feature being that it's faster than anything else on the road, but you could easily upgrade it to being fairly damage resistance via Matter). Bullet-resistant suits or coats, especially for MiBs, is probably fitting. Ray guns may be a little too vulgar for the Union's tastes, but are probably still an option during emergencies. It's probably a little too soon for power armor, as that isn't really a thing until the late 50s. The idea of replacing body parts with artificial ones is an idea from the time. Electric brain stimulators. Emergency medical regeneration tanks. Microsurgery tools. And various miracle drugs that can (temporarily) eliminate the need to eat, sleep, or go to the bathroom, or put you into a hibernating sleep state.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-12T22:29:27-07:00","id":1451947,"title":"","content":"Comic books and comic strips of the era (and derivative media like movie serials) would likely provide a good example of advanced technology that would be available as how it looks. You can find three Flash Gordon movie serials in the public domain that show all sorts of advance science and technology as imagined by people at the time. It's a good example to see what they though the future would be like, and how much they completely missed what it would be like.<br /> <br /> Another easy solution would be to take distinctive jet age technology from the 1960s and have it be in the 1940s. The 1960s was still very much an analog technology age. Simple transistors appeared to be miracle that could do anything. The early Iron Man comics of Stan Lee always used the term &quot;transistor&quot; to explain Iron Man's power like it was magic.<br /> <br /> (I should have read No One of Consequence's post earlier, as he pretty much mentioned everything I did and more.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 06-13-2021, 01:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T04:32:04-07:00","id":1451976,"title":"","content":"Think about drugs a lot. Captain America was transformed by a drug. Hourman has to continually use &quot;miraco&quot; his miracle drug to get his powers ( bunches of supers from Red Rocket to the French comic superhero Astrix needed a fix for superpowers). In House of Dracula the doctor is using a drug to reshape bone. A drug both makes the Invisible Man invisible and drives him insane. Heck a mad scientist used a drug to turn a ghost into a living woman in a Three Stooges short. Drugs were supertools in the popular imagination of the period.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T06:37:04-07:00","id":1451985,"title":"","content":"Bear in mind that the Void Engineers always operate on a more out-there basis than the rest of the Union. Power Armor (first introduced in Heinlein's Starship Troopers, IIRC) <i>are</i> a little premature even for them; the novel is from the late 50s. But ray guns and force fields? The rest of the Union might not be ready for them; but the Void Engineers would be. Also some psionics: this is the time of the Lensman series' publication.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T07:23:42-07:00","id":1451993,"title":"","content":"Dataweaver has a major point &quot;Mental Powers&quot; and &quot;Mind over Matter&quot; were common tropes in period. Think of all those pulp heroes with mind powers that learned the &quot;secrets of Tibet!&quot; If you read the serious literature of the early 20th century, you'll notice that it's assumed that sometime in the future humanity will be telepathic and clairvoyant. So the Technocracy would definitely have &quot;the power to cloud men's minds!&quot; But once Television became popular they saw it was redundant.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T08:11:39-07:00","id":1451998,"title":"","content":"The reason I divide up function and aesthetic, though, is that while people may not have anticipated cell phones and the internet and the like, the Technocracy did and actively pushed for them. Assume everything we have today and everything we anticipate tomorrow is something the Technocracy was pushing in the 40s, just with a different look, Assume also that they give special attention to communications networks, supply chains, and medicine.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T09:03:40-07:00","id":1452002,"title":"","content":"As far as the Progenitors go, eugenics will still be something of an influence. While it's already starting to be socially discredited because of the German National Socialists (with, for example, HG Wells renouncing his previous support of the concept by 1940), there is always that certain type of enlightened expert who will refuse to abandon ideas or movements they've based much of their professional career on. On the flip side, this period, from the 30s to the very early 50s, is when a lot of our current understanding of DNA and how it worked were first developed, including some of the early science fiction ideas about genetic engineering. And as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar857_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"857\" data-vbnamecard=\"857\">Astromancer</a> mentions, this is the era of &quot;wonder drugs&quot;, most especially the real world development and mass production of penicillin and sulfa drugs. <br /> <br /> And as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a> mentions, the Void Engineers, especially those operating beyond the Gauntlet, probably go the full Buck Rogers route, complete with laser guns and rocket packs. And if you've never seen it, I do recommend the Colonel Weird material from Jeff Lemire's Black Hammer comics, especially the Cosmagog miniseries, as potential period Void Engineer inspiration. <br /> <br /> Regarding psychic abilities and parapsychology, this is just after the era of places like Stanford and Duke Universities trying to study things like ESP and psychokinesis in a laboratory setting, and getting what could best be described as mixed results. Scientific interest in psychic phenomenon fell off a bit during the War, but started to pick up again in the 50s. To a certain degree, the Progenitors are likely to continue studying it, especially once they can try to genetically engineer potential subjects. However, this is probably sort of a back burner project at least until the 50s. (On the other hand, the 30s pulp style of psychical abilities is certainly still very much a thing for the period Sons of Ether.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T09:32:27-07:00","id":1452005,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1451650-1940s-hypertech?p=1451998#post1451998\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The reason I divide up function and aesthetic, though, is that while people may not have anticipated cell phones and the internet and the like, the Technocracy did and actively pushed for them. Assume everything we have today and everything we anticipate tomorrow is something the Technocracy was pushing in the 40s, just with a different look, Assume also that they give special attention to communications networks, supply chains, and medicine.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sort of. The 1940s were still a bit early for anything related to the Internet, even when dealing with the Virtual Adepts: they didn't actually discover the Digital Web until a decade later. From their founding in the 1830s until then, they were primarily occupied with the mechanics of computation, and had not yet made the connection with Correspondence or the notion of a digital network or distributed computing. They were getting close in the 1940s; but they weren't quite there yet. <br /> <br /> So the Internet doesn't actually have a place in 1940s Hypertech. The Technocracy <i>wasn't</i> pushing it; and when the Virtual Adepts finally got around to pushing it a decade later, that coincided with their departure from the Union. Like quantum physics (which canonically was introduced by the Sons of Ether after <i>their</i> departure from the Union), it's an innovation that Mage doesn't credit to the Technocracy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 06-13-2021, 12:34 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T18:03:49-07:00","id":1452096,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1451650-1940s-hypertech?p=1451985#post1451985\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Bear in mind that the Void Engineers always operate on a more out-there basis than the rest of the Union. Power Armor (first introduced in Heinlein's Starship Troopers, IIRC) <i>are</i> a little premature even for them; the novel is from the late 50s. But ray guns and force fields? The rest of the Union might not be ready for them; but the Void Engineers would be. Also some psionics: this is the time of the Lensman series' publication.</div> </div> </div> </div>The needle to thread is that period pulp fiction should influence the performative aspects of Technocratic magic and the aesthetic, not necessarily the functionality. In-universe, all this pulp fiction would ostensibly work on a very subtle level to influence the direction the Technocracy attempts to guide the Consensus; remember, we're still talking about Hypertech, which is technology the Technocracy has already invented, which still lies outside the reach of the Consensus and is ultimately what the Technocracy is trying to guide humanity into.<br /> <br /> Take for instance what we should assume of the Manhattan Project, as an analog to Alan Turing's in-universe work with computing and artificial intelligence. The Technocrats almost certainly had the atom bomb, or at least schematics and working prototypes which may have even been tested far, far away from prying Sleeper eyes; the challenge was to develop, prototype, and test it by proxy in such a way the damn thing would work with Witnesses around. And, they likely would have had it years if not decades prior to the Trinity test. And spinning off that, one may not be too far from the mark arguing Castle Bravo would be a case example of a Paradox backlash in pushing the Consensus too far, too fast, in terms of nuclear technology.<br /> <br /> As far as power armor goes, hell the Order of Reason had power armor in the 1400's. There are two or three wonders in TSC that are implicitly -- not outright stated to be, but for all intents and purposes are -- power armor, either in the form of exoskeletal frames or full-on suits. It was shit, because it was clockwork crap in the vein of HIT Mark II clockwork automata, but it was there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T18:36:07-07:00","id":1452100,"title":"","content":"The 1940s equivalent to the Internet is going to be radio. Besides the typical broadcast uses we're still used to today, this includes shortwave radio communications, AT&amp;T's very early Mobile Telephone Service (first introduced in 1947), and the early experiments (as far back as the 20s) in using it to send visible television images. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T04:41:54-07:00","id":1452144,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1451650-1940s-hypertech?p=1452005#post1452005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Sort of. The 1940s were still a bit early for anything related to the Internet, even when dealing with the Virtual Adepts: they didn't actually discover the Digital Web until a decade later. From their founding in the 1830s until then, they were primarily occupied with the mechanics of computation, and had not yet made the connection with Correspondence or the notion of a digital network or distributed computing. They were getting close in the 1940s; but they weren't quite there yet.<br /> <br /> So the Internet doesn't actually have a place in 1940s Hypertech. The Technocracy <i>wasn't</i> pushing it; and when the Virtual Adepts finally got around to pushing it a decade later, that coincided with their departure from the Union. Like quantum physics (which canonically was introduced by the Sons of Ether after <i>their</i> departure from the Union), it's an innovation that Mage doesn't credit to the Technocracy.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> True, whatever they were pushing would not look like the modern internet, especially if we bring otherworldly elements like the Digital Web into it. That said, the sheer utility of interconnected data systems had to be on their radar. Likely it would look more like the old ARPANET and have a significantly higher focus on security. <br /> <br /> It is often said that the first thing people do with any new technology is make weapons, but historically the place of pride more often goes to communications and information gathering. I figure the Technocracy is no different.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T04:51:54-07:00","id":1452145,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1451650-1940s-hypertech?p=1452100#post1452100\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The 1940s equivalent to the Internet is going to be radio. Besides the typical broadcast uses we're still used to today, this includes shortwave radio communications, AT&amp;T's very early Mobile Telephone Service (first introduced in 1947), and the early experiments (as far back as the 20s) in using it to send visible television images.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> A fun and misleading trivia question is this: What was the first cartoon character broadcast on television and when did it happen?<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Wording matters a lot here. The first <i>character</i> broadcast was Felix the Cat. In 1928 a rotating statue of him was broadcast as part of an experiment. The high contrast between the black and white parts of the character made him ideal for the test.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Given that both cable and broadcast technology already existed in the 40s, I would assume the hypertech of the Technocracy at that point was well beyond.<br /> <br /> Edit: As a side element, the history of radio and television in America is a pitched battle between free expression and government control/social standards and involves several major Supreme Court cases. It is a gold mine for ways the Technocracy might advance its agenda over the years.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":758,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","user":"Ramnesis","date":". 06-14-2021, 08:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T07:12:22-07:00","id":1452162,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1451650-1940s-hypertech?p=1452002#post1452002\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As far as the Progenitors go, eugenics will still be something of an influence. While it's already starting to be socially discredited because of the German National Socialists (with, for example, HG Wells renouncing his previous support of the concept by 1940)...this is the era of &quot;wonder drugs&quot;, most especially the real world development and mass production of penicillin and sulfa drugs.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1451650-1940s-hypertech?p=1452145#post1452145\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Edit: As a side element, the history of radio and television in America is a pitched battle between free expression and government control/social standards and involves several major Supreme Court cases. It is a gold mine for ways the Technocracy might advance its agenda over the years.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm kludging these two posts together to make a bit of a point; that could and perhaps ought to be, Technocratic political interests need not operate on an exclusively domestic level. Look also to how the Technocracy might exploit international politics, particularly the Cold War, to advance the Consensus and execute the Pogrom. A good example, since the Progenitors came up, being the consumption of the race to create and distribute the polio vaccine by the Cold War.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Regarding psychic abilities and parapsychology, this is just after the era of places like Stanford and Duke Universities trying to study things like ESP and psychokinesis in a laboratory setting, and getting what could best be described as mixed results. Scientific interest in psychic phenomenon fell off a bit during the War, but started to pick up again in the 50s. To a certain degree, the Progenitors are likely to continue studying it, especially once they can try to genetically engineer potential subjects. However, this is probably sort of a back burner project at least until the 50s. (On the other hand, the 30s pulp style of psychical abilities is certainly still very much a thing for the period Sons of Ether.) </div> </div> </div>I'd exercise caution and subtlety with this topic, due to how closely it's tied into Nazism, the Cold War, and the dark side of domestic national security policy. This was, after all, the age of Operation Paperclip, MKULTRA and other less prominent mass medical/chemical/bioweapons testing on the American public without consent or knowledge, COINTELPRO and Operation Mockingbird, and the like. For lack of a more sensitive way to say it, that's far more the NWO's gig as the prime executors of the Pogrom than any other Convention, including the Progenitors. For ill or worse, Nazi Germany's rise and fall, and the Nazi scientists and researchers smuggled from the country and given new identities to continue their work, provided a case example and wealth of knowledge for the Convention to increase the efficiency and effectiveness of the Pogrom, and manipulate the Sleepers.<br /> <br /> Were I to run a Technocratic chronicle during the early Cold War period, I'd certainly make the &quot;Operation Paperclip and second Red Scare&quot; era of the early Cold War a key nexus for Nephandic infiltration of the Technocracy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":431,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","user":"Theodrim","date":". 06-14-2021, 10:16 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T09:40:41-07:00","id":1452176,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1451650-1940s-hypertech?p=1452162#post1452162\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm kludging these two posts together to make a bit of a point; that could and perhaps ought to be, Technocratic political interests need not operate on an exclusively domestic level. Look also to how the Technocracy might exploit international politics, particularly the Cold War, to advance the Consensus and execute the Pogrom. A good example, since the Progenitors came up, being the consumption of the race to create and distribute the polio vaccine by the Cold War.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well certainly, but I have only looked into the history of television for the US so that is the only area I know well enough to recommend. Also, the American television industry has a far wider reach than just within the US itself. <br /> <br /> All that said, for the purposes of the 40's I would focus more on the end of WWII and the rise of the Cold War than I would on the nascent television industry. Less television stations and more numbers stations.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T13:15:41-07:00","id":1452212,"title":"","content":"This has been incredibly helpful, people. Words are not enough to express how grateful I am for your help. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5394-argonot","name":"Argonot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5394_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"227"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T15:00:51-07:00","id":1452241,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Argonot</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1451650-1940s-hypertech?p=1452212#post1452212\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This has been incredibly helpful, people. Words are not enough to express how grateful I am for your help. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Glad to help. Hope you can give us an update at some point. <br /> Which part of the 40s are you focusing on, btw, the War years or the aftermath? (Or both?)<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T15:30:13-07:00","id":1452243,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1451650-1940s-hypertech?p=1452145#post1452145\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Given that both cable and broadcast technology already existed in the 40s, I would assume the hypertech of the Technocracy at that point was well beyond.<br /> <br /> Edit: As a side element, the history of radio and television in America is a pitched battle between free expression and government control/social standards and involves several major Supreme Court cases. It is a gold mine for ways the Technocracy might advance its agenda over the years.</div> </div> </div> </div>Quite a few 1930s films have TV surveillance cameras. The most famous would be &quot;The Werewolf of London&quot; and &quot;Flash Gordon.&quot; The Technocracy would clearly have plenty of spy cameras. <br /> <br /> Britain had the first TV broadcasts for audiences that I know of, 1935. I think Germany was the only nation that had TV broadcasts throughout the war. <br /> <br /> Also frequency modulation (FM) was invented in the later 1940s. Thus FM radio broadcasts would be secret broadcasts no one would notice in the 1930s or the war years.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-16T06:47:57-07:00","id":1452466,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1451650-1940s-hypertech?p=1452241#post1452241\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Glad to help. Hope you can give us an update at some point.<br /> Which part of the 40s are you focusing on, btw, the War years or the aftermath? (Or both?)<br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> My chronicle is about the arrival of the Technocracy to a South American country. We started in 1940 and are about to start 1944. I use &quot;radio news reports&quot; about the war as part of the background and mood of the story, but it is not directly related to what happens to the players. The chronicle will finish in 1948 (when there's a Riot which basically destroys the heart of the city they're playing in).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5394-argonot","name":"Argonot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5394_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"227"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}